{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Only he shall rise.</b> We rely on the Sages, who interpreted it as 'on his staff' — that he does not lean on another as sick people do, but only on himself." "<b>and the striker shall be cleared.</b> That they bring him out from prison." "<b>and his rest.</b> From the root שביתה, in that his work ceased. It is correct. And it is on the pattern of שְׁבָרוֹ (Genesis 44:2), for the tav is a root letter. And it is possible that it is like וְשָׁבַת הַבַּיִת מְעַט (Ruth 2:7), which is from the root ישב, the meaning being the time of sitting in the house, idle from his work. And the proof for this interpretation is that it is the opposite of וְהִתְהַלֵּךְ בַּחוּץ (he walked about outside)." "<b>and shall cause him to be thoroughly healed.</b> As a sign that permission was given to physicians to heal wounds and bruises that they see on the outside; but every illness that is internal, in the body, is in the hand of HaShem to heal it. Thus it is written, כִּי הוּא יַכְאִיב וְיַחְבֵּשׁ (Job 5:18), and it is written of Asa, גַּם בַּחֲלֵיו לֹא דָרַשׁ אֶת־יְהוָה כִּי אִם־בָּרֹפְאִים (II Chronicles 16:12). Now, Scripture distinguished, for it did not say וְרָפָא from the light binyan, but וְרַפֹּא יְרַפֵּא, which is from the heavy binyan. And I will explain this further in this parashah. And our Sages received other things besides both of these, as they received בַּשָּׁמִים עִם קְטֹרֶת סַמִּים, and they are not written."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:37:14.703536052Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}